{
  "families": [
    {
      "family": "unrolled_k",
      "ranges": [
        {"param": "data_width", "low": 14, "high": 18, "choices": null},
        {"param": "n_iter", "low": 12, "high": 20, "choices": null},
        {"param": "angle_guard", "low": -1, "high": 3, "choices": null},
        {"param": "frac_guard", "low": 0, "high": 3, "choices": null},
        {"param": "rounding", "low": null, "high": null, "choices": ["trunc", "round"]},
        {"param": "k", "low": 4, "high": 8, "choices": null}
      ],
      "budget_share": 0.8,
      "why": "This is the only family I hold that can plausibly satisfy the burst constraint. The real binding constraint is sys_p99_batch_us <= 0.5 with 32 simultaneous requests, i.e. an effective >=64 results/us, twice the nominal 32 Msps. unrolled_k gives ceil(N/k)+3 cycles/result, so with the accuracy-required N>=13 a k>=4 keeps the required Fmax in a range sky130 can plausibly reach, while k=8 minimises cycles at the cost of a longer combinational chain and more area. I sweep k=4..8 to expose exactly where the Fmax/area/cycles trade-off crosses the 0.5us batch bound, and N=12..20 to find the smallest N that still meets max_abs_err<=2^-12. data_width starts at 14 because the output LSB is 2^-(W-2): W=14 with round rounding gives half-LSB 2^-13 < 2^-12, so W<14 cannot meet the error bound. angle_guard/frac_guard are swept to buy internal precision cheaply before paying for extra output width, and rounding is swept because round vs trunc is the difference between W=14 passing or failing."
    },
    {
      "family": "iterative",
      "ranges": [
        {"param": "data_width", "low": 14, "high": 18, "choices": null},
        {"param": "n_iter", "low": 12, "high": 20, "choices": null},
        {"param": "angle_guard", "low": -1, "high": 3, "choices": null},
        {"param": "frac_guard", "low": 0, "high": 3, "choices": null},
        {"param": "rounding", "low": null, "high": null, "choices": ["trunc", "round"]}
      ],
      "budget_share": 0.2,
      "why": "Smallest-area family, so it would win the min-area selection if it were feasible, and it is worth a small share to confirm or refute that rather than assume it. It needs N+3 cycles/result, so the 64 results/us burst requirement forces Fmax >= 64*(N+3); with the N>=13 that accuracy demands that is ~1 GHz, which I expect to be out of reach, but the cost model must tell me that, not me. I keep the same accuracy-relevant ranges as unrolled_k (W>=14 for the 2^-12 output-LSB bound, N=12..20, guards, rounding) so that if any iterative point does clear the batch bound it lands on the same accuracy axis and merges cleanly into the front."
    }
  ],
  "rationale": "The spec's hard constraints are max_abs_err <= 2^-12 and sys_p99_batch_us <= 0.5 for 32 requests arriving together every 1us. The batch constraint is the real driver: 32 results in 0.5us means >=64 results/us, double the nominal throughput_msps>=32, and it is what separates the two families I own. Accuracy pins the search space: output LSB is 2^-(W-2), so W>=14 is needed for 2^-12 (W=14 only with round rounding), and the CORDIC angle residual needs N>=13. Those accuracy-required N values are exactly what makes the iterative family's N+3 cycles/result expensive against the burst bound, whereas unrolled_k can trade k against Fmax. I therefore put 80% of the budget on unrolled_k, concentrated on the k=4..8 / N=12..20 boundary where the feasible min-area design (the selection rule is min area_um2) will sit, and 20% on iterative to verify infeasibility or catch a surprise. Both families share identical accuracy parameters, so any feasible points merge onto one front."
}

{
  "decision": "add_family",
  "rationale": "Every evaluation so far violates throughput_msps>=32 (best 9.43) and sys_p99_batch_us<=0.5 (best 3.38, i.e. ~7x over). This is structural, not a tuning problem: the system scenario issues 32 requests at one tick and needs all 32 back within 0.5 us, i.e. a burst rate of ~64 MSPS. Both explored families are multi-cycle-per-result by construction (iterative: N+3 cycles/result; unrolled_k: ceil(N/k)+3 cycles/result), so their throughput is Fmax/(cycles per result) and the observed ceiling of 9.43 MSPS is exactly that wall. No amount of narrowing data_width/n_iter/guard tuning can move a 1-result-per-N-cycles datapath to 64 MSPS. The registry's pipelined and pipelined_m families emit 1 result/cycle, so at any plausible Fmax they clear both the 32 MSPS average and the 32-results-in-0.5us burst bound. Accuracy is family-independent (identical bit-accurate datapath), so the accuracy constraint (best seen 13.90 bits, need >=12) is already satisfiable and will carry over. The remaining budget (40 evals) should go to the two unexplored 1-result/cycle families.",
  "next_plan": {
    "families": [
      {
        "family": "pipelined",
        "ranges": [
          {"param": "data_width", "low": 14, "high": 20, "choices": null},
          {"param": "n_iter", "low": 12, "high": 20, "choices": null},
          {"param": "angle_guard", "low": 0, "high": 3, "choices": null},
          {"param": "frac_guard", "low": 0, "high": 3, "choices": null},
          {"param": "rounding", "low": null, "high": null, "choices": ["trunc", "round"]}
        ]
      ,
        "budget_share": 0.5
      ,
        "why": "1 result/cycle; N+2 latency. data_width>=14 with n_iter>=12 already reaches >12 accuracy bits in the iterative data (13.90 bits at W=16/N=13), so the accuracy constraint is met while area stays small. Keep W low (14-20) because area is the objective."
      },
      {
        "family": "pipelined_m",
        "ranges": [
          {"param": "data_width", "low": 14, "high": 20, "choices": null},
          {"param": "n_iter", "low": 12, "high": 20, "choices": null},
          {"param": "angle_guard", "low": 0, "high": 3, "choices": null},
          {"param": "frac_guard", "low": 0, "high": 3, "choices": null},
          {"param": "rounding", "low": null, "high": null, "choices": ["trunc", "round"]},
          {"param": "m", "low": 2, "high": 8, "choices": null}
        ]
      ,
        "budget_share": 0.5
      ,
        "why": "Also 1 result/cycle but registers every m stages, so it trades Fmax against FF count/area; m is the knob that may let a smaller-area design still clear the 0.5 us batch bound. Include it so the front covers the area/accuracy trade-off, not just the pipelined corner."
      }
    ],
    "rationale": "Both families are 1-result/cycle, so the throughput and sys_p99_batch constraints become reachable for the first time; the search then reduces to minimising area subject to max_abs_err<=2^-12, which the bit-accurate model already shows is achievable at W~14-16, N~12-14. Ranges are kept narrow and low-width because area is the objective and the accuracy headroom is already demonstrated."
  }
}
